    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, i'm looking at this NDA for a fintech client and the \"residuals\" section is a bit confusing. can you help me understand what this actually means in plain english? \n\n\"Notwithstanding anything to the contrary in this Agreement, the Receiving Party shall be free to use for any purpose the Residuals resulting from access to or use of the Confidential Information, provided that the Receiving Party shall not disclose the Confidential Information itself. The term 'Residuals' as used in this Section means information in non-tangible form, which may be retained in the unaided memory of persons who have had access to the Confidential Information, including ideas, concepts, know-how, or techniques retained during the course of the engagement.\"\n\nbasically, does this mean i can use the coding patterns or general logic i learn while working for them in my next job?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, this section allows you to use the general concepts like coding patterns or logic you learned, provided you are not using any specific, identifiable methods or proprietary code that belongs to them. The term \"Residuals\" specifically refers to abstract ideas retained in your memory after you leave the organization, which are not protected because they lack tangible form. However, you generally cannot apply these ideas in a way that helps them or their competitors, as reasonable legal principles may still limit how broadly you can transfer knowledge obtained during the engagement.",
